module std where

-- =========================================================================
-- Int (sign-magnitude) arithmetic — the PURE source of truth (numeric-tower
-- Phase 2).
--
-- These bodies are the conceptual definition of integer arithmetic over the
-- pure ADT `Int = Int magnitude: Nat negative: Bool` (the sign-magnitude
-- representation; type-foundation.ts:577 / fenel/std/foundation.fenel). They
-- COMPOSE the Phase-1 Nat bodies (natAdd/natMul/natMonus/natLt/natEq, over
-- `Zero | Succ`): an Int's magnitude IS a Nat, so same-sign addition is natAdd
-- of magnitudes, opposite-sign is natMonus + natLt of magnitudes, multiply is
-- natMul of magnitudes with an xor of signs. They are the SEMANTICS that the
-- flat `@native` `add`/`sub`/`mul`/`negate`/`lt`/`eq` over the opaque `number`
-- repr (math-native.fenel / logic-native.fenel + json-logic.ts) are the runtime
-- performance branch FOR, and that the compile DirectOp lowering (`add→F64Add`
-- … lower-to-wasm-ast.fenel / lower-to-llvm-ast.fenel) is the compiled perf
-- branch FOR. The D7 round-trip law (numeric-tower notes/phase-0-audit.md §3)
-- binds all three. [[feedback_opaque_is_optimization]]
--
-- NAMED-RECORD SURFACE. Int's pure is a single-case `$record` with two
-- meaningful fields, so these bodies build + destructure via the NAMED surface
-- (`Int { magnitude = m, negative = n }` / `case a of Int { magnitude = m,
-- negative = n } -> …`) — far clearer than the positional `_0`/`_1`. The
-- construct gap is dissolved (representation-substrate Phases 1–2): registering
-- `Int` as a catalog entity (the `@label`/`@icon`/`@description` on its
-- foundation.fenel decl) makes the registry `$opaque`-peel index
-- `_constructors.Int = ["magnitude", "negative"]`, so the named surface is
-- unambiguous (the named-vs-positional construct logic keys a plain-object
-- payload whose keys match the field set as NAMED regardless, but registration
-- makes it principled). ZERO new TS — the bodies are pure `.fenel`.
--
-- THE NEGATIVE-ZERO HAZARD (the central sign-magnitude design problem).
-- Sign-magnitude has TWO zeros: `Int { magnitude = Zero, negative = False }`
-- (+0) and `Int { magnitude = Zero, negative = True }` (−0). If −0 escapes,
-- structural `intEq` breaks (+0 ≠ −0 structurally), the D7 law splits, and one
-- mathematical value forks in two. THE RESOLUTION: a normalizing smart
-- constructor `mkInt` that FORCES +0 (a `Zero` magnitude always yields
-- `negative = False`). EVERY op builds its result through `mkInt`, never a bare
-- `Int { … }` literal in result position. Then −0 is unrepresentable
-- post-construction, the bridges produce +0 naturally, and
-- `intNegate (mkInt Zero _)` stays +0. Raw typed pure records can still carry
-- the other zero sign; equality and order normalize its effective sign too.
--
-- These remain Fenel-bodied Int-typed fnIds (`std:mkInt`/`std:intAdd` …).
-- `integer-ffi.ts` provides their law-bound exact representation shortcut;
-- genuinely pure inputs continue through these bodies. The existing `Numeric
-- Int` methods reference these same functions. No Number native or DirectOp
-- lowering changes. `intDiv`/`intMod`
-- are deferred (the roadmap Phase-2 row omits them; Int truncated-vs-floored
-- division has its own sign-rule design — built when Rational/solver forces it).

-- --- sign-flag combination helpers (over the `negative` Bool) ---------------
-- `sameSign`/`xorSign` operate on the two `negative` flags. Kept as int-domain-
-- named helpers (NOT general `std:boolEq`/`boolXor`, to avoid claiming a std
-- boolean-namespace a future Eq-class phase may want) so intAdd/intMul/intEq
-- read as the algorithm, not the boolean algebra. Both bottom out at the std
-- word-operators (and/or/not), which lower to the construct perf branch.

@label "Sign flags equal"
@description "True when two `negative` sign flags agree (both negative or both non-negative). Helper for intAdd (same-sign test) and intEq. `sameSign a b = or (and a b) (and (not a) (not b))`."
@tags "numeric" "int" "sign"
sameSign : (a: Bool) -> (b: Bool) -> Boolean
sameSign a b = or (and (boolToNative a) (boolToNative b)) (and (not (boolToNative a)) (not (boolToNative b)))

@label "Sign flags xor"
@description "Exclusive-or of two `negative` sign flags — the sign of a product (negative iff exactly one operand is negative). Helper for intMul. `xorSign a b = or (and a (not b)) (and (not a) b)`."
@tags "numeric" "int" "sign"
xorSign : (a: Bool) -> (b: Bool) -> Boolean
xorSign a b = or (and (boolToNative a) (not (boolToNative b))) (and (not (boolToNative a)) (boolToNative b))

-- --- the normalizer + the ops -----------------------------------------------

@label "Make Int"
@description "The normalizing smart constructor — the negative-zero guard. mkInt m neg builds `Int { magnitude = m, negative = neg }` with the mathematical Bool flag, EXCEPT a Zero magnitude always yields `negative = False` (forcing +0). Every arithmetic result routes through mkInt so −0 is unrepresentable post-construction. Equality and order also handle raw pure constructor zero signs mathematically. An ordinary Fenel body — a case on the magnitude being Zero."
@tags "numeric" "int" "sign-magnitude"
mkInt : (m: Nat) -> (neg: Bool) -> Int
mkInt m neg = case m of
  Zero -> Int { magnitude = Zero, negative = False }
  Succ p -> Int { magnitude = m, negative = boolToNative neg }

@label "Int negate"
@description "Arithmetic negation — flip the sign flag through mkInt so −0 ⇒ +0: intNegate (Int m neg) = mkInt m (not neg). A tower op (the Nat rung has no negation; Int is the first SIGNED rung). Total."
@tags "numeric" "int" "unary"
intNegate : (a: Int) -> Int
intNegate a = case a of
  Int { magnitude = m, negative = n } -> mkInt m (not (boolToNative n))

@label "Int add"
@description "Signed addition by case split on the operand signs. SAME sign ⇒ mkInt (natAdd |a| |b|) sign — add magnitudes, keep the shared sign. OPPOSITE sign ⇒ subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger with natMonus and take the LARGER's sign (natLt picks the larger). Equal opposite magnitudes ⇒ natMonus = Zero ⇒ mkInt forces +0 (no special case needed). Composes the Phase-1 Nat ops + mkInt."
@tags "numeric" "int"
intAdd : (a: Int) -> (b: Int) -> Int
intAdd a b = case a of
  Int { magnitude = m1, negative = n1 } -> case b of
    Int { magnitude = m2, negative = n2 } -> if sameSign n1 n2 then mkInt (natAdd m1 m2) n1 else if natLt m1 m2 then mkInt (natMonus m2 m1) n2 else mkInt (natMonus m1 m2) n1

@label "Int subtract"
@description "Signed subtraction — now TOTAL (the Nat rung's natMonus truncated at Zero; Int is where subtraction becomes total/signed). intSub a b = intAdd a (intNegate b)."
@tags "numeric" "int"
intSub : (a: Int) -> (b: Int) -> Int
intSub a b = intAdd a (intNegate b)

@label "Int multiply"
@description "Signed multiplication — magnitude is natMul of the magnitudes, sign is the xor of the operand signs (negative iff exactly one is negative): mkInt (natMul |a| |b|) (xorSign n1 n2). A zero magnitude ⇒ natMul = Zero ⇒ mkInt forces +0 (so 0 × negative = +0). Composes natMul + mkInt."
@tags "numeric" "int"
intMul : (a: Int) -> (b: Int) -> Int
intMul a b = case a of
  Int { magnitude = m1, negative = n1 } -> case b of
    Int { magnitude = m2, negative = n2 } -> mkInt (natMul m1 m2) (xorSign n1 n2)

@label "Int less-than"
@description "Sign-aware strict order. A zero magnitude has a non-negative effective sign even when a raw pure constructor carries True. a negative, b non-negative ⇒ True; a non-negative, b negative ⇒ False; both non-negative ⇒ natLt of magnitudes; both negative ⇒ natLt REVERSED (−3 < −2 iff |−3| > |−2|). Total (Int order is decidable; the ℝ rung is where lt goes partial, D4)."
@tags "numeric" "int" "comparison"
intLt : (a: Int) -> (b: Int) -> Boolean
intLt a b = case a of
  Int { magnitude = m1, negative = n1 } -> case b of
    Int { magnitude = m2, negative = n2 } ->
      let negative1 = and (boolToNative n1) (not (natEq m1 Zero))
      let negative2 = and (boolToNative n2) (not (natEq m2 Zero))
      if negative1 then (if negative2 then natLt m2 m1 else True) else (if negative2 then False else natLt m1 m2)

@label "Int equal"
@description "Mathematical equality — equal magnitudes and equal effective signs. Both zero magnitudes are equal regardless of the raw constructor's sign flag; nonzero magnitudes require matching signs. Total. The pure source of truth for `eq` at the Int rung."
@tags "numeric" "int" "comparison"
intEq : (a: Int) -> (b: Int) -> Boolean
intEq a b = case a of
  Int { magnitude = m1, negative = n1 } -> case b of
    Int { magnitude = m2, negative = n2 } -> if natEq m1 m2 then (if natEq m1 Zero then True else sameSign n1 n2) else False
